 to the sky  they'll tell you when certain things  rise and set on the horizon where  they're going to be and these will be  very helpful in planning what you would  like to take pictures of the next one  also a phone application or on your  computer it's check the weather  especially our by our forecasts you can  see that actually this is the forecast  from today when I'm recording the video  it's actually going to be very clear  tonight which is great for  astrophotography you want to avoid  obviously clouds and rain so plan ahead  the next thing that I recommend is a  flashlight now not just any flashlights  if possible use a red flashlight red  flashlights do not ruin your night  vision as much it takes a long time for  your eyes to adjust and you'll notice  the longer you spend outside the more  stars you're just going to be able to  see with the naked eye so by using a red  flashlight you'll still be able to light  things in order to adjust camera  settings or whatever you need to do but  also not ruin that night vision that you  work so hard to get  lastly astrophotography does take  patience we're going to be taking some  long exposure photos we're going to be  taking lots of images we'll be waiting  for clouds to pass we'll be waiting for  things to come up above the horizon and  out of the way of trees so even during  the summer it gets kind of cold at night  dress warmly so bring some warm clothes  sweatshirt gloves whatever you need  because it will get chilly out there  now that you have what you need to to  start shooting let's start talking about  our camera settings for our exposure  triangle um the first thing we're going  to talk about is aperture now with  astrophotography because everything  we're photographing is so far away what  we say it's all at infinity we don't  have to worry about depth of field so we  can shoot with a very wide aperture or  your lowest aperture settings F 1.4 2.8  3.5 whatever the lowest you can set if  you're using an interchangeable lens  camera by having a wide aperture that  will also help you capture more  let more photons enter your camera to  give you a brighter image now with that  though because your depth of field is  low and you're focusing on something far  away focus is very important so whether  you're using a cell phone and a point  shoot interchangeable lens camera it's  really easy to get out of focus stars  and I've had lots of nights when I've  gone out to shoot and I come back to  look at my images and all my hard work I  end up with these types of blurry images  and it's because I was slightly out of  focus so what I recommend in order to  make sure you have these pinpoint stars  is to use manual focus and take lots of  test shots so turn off the auto focus on  your camera you're going to use the  manual focus setting focus it look in  the camera try to get them sharp take a  picture then zoom in and check to see if  your star turn focus if they're not  adjust and try again and do that a few  times to make sure that when you start  spending a lot of time taking your  photos you get really sharp stars  now the next settings we're going to  talk about is the shutter speed now in  order to capture the most light as  possible and get a bright image you're  going to want to use a fairly long  shutter speed when you use long shutter  speeds because you're capturing more and  more light you're going to start being  able to see all these dim stars that you  may not be able to see with the naked  eye so you may discover something new  stars just by using this long shutter  speed now when you do this I recommend  that you use a shutter speed that is no  longer than 30 seconds to keep your  stars as pinpoints so keep your image  really sharp now the reason why I say 30  seconds is that the earth is spinning  and the earth is spinning once around  its axis every 24 hours and what that  causes is motion blur so you can see in  this image this isn't due to camera  shake but 