y really well for long  exposures here's another one that we  built using cardboard and this by taping  the edges here in different places you  can adjust the angle that your phone is  pointing to also aim your camera in a  third example here is using a cardboard  tube where I've cut out slots here for  the phone the phone sits here and then  I've waited it down it happens that this  tube fits right in the tape and the tape  is holding this down to the table to  make a stable tripod for larger  interchangeable-lens cameras you can  actually use this similar system so in  this case I'm actually using the exact  same cardboard stand that used for the  cell phone but just prop the camera up  on the other side and here's the picture  in the middle here is the the back view  of that and we're using this tape here  to provide a little extra stability to  the camera this lets me access all the  controls to the camera while keeping the  camera in the same place in the last  example here it's similar using  cardboard but this design allows us to  weight down our stand a little bit too  and we're using some coins here to keep  our camera pointed in the direction that  we want again super important when  you're using these long exposure times  make sure that you have a stable tripod  or your pictures your going to be blurry  the last setting that we're going to  look at is ISO now for your ISO higher  values are going to allow you to capture  more detail especially if we're looking  at things like the Milky Way but it also  adds more noise so you're going to want  to pick an ISO based on your particular  camera and how much noise your camera is  going to produce this will also be  affected as how by how much light  pollution is around because a higher ISO  not only makes your camera  more easily detect like from the stars  but also light from around you so you  can see in this picture here I use the  high ISO which allowed the stars up at  the top of the image to be nice and  