 for each of those photos  it was probably about a hundred photos  here was 30 seconds and you could  actually even go a bit longer and by  using a long shutter speed it allows the  stars and the images to move a little  bit and you get a little bit of motion  blur in each image and many cameras have  what are called intervalometer z' built  in these let you choose the starter  speed of your camera and how often you  want your camera to take a photo so you  don't have to stand outside for the full  two hours so you can set up the  intervalometer point your camera focus  it start it go inside and draw some hot  chocolate and then go out and check your  camera every once in a while just to  make sure that your battery isn't dying  and once you have all of those images  let me show you what they look like  so you can see as I take the images over  these two hours the stars are circling  around that North Star so we can now  insert these into there's a lot of free  software out there in this case I'm  using one called star Stax and when I  put this in the software and click stack  you can see that it's going to blend  these images together in order to create  consistent trails across the sky this is  why you want a little bit of blur in  your photo a little bit of that motion  blur to help connect those images  together you can see in a couple of  points in the image you can see some  dots and that's because my shutter speed  was probably just not quite long enough  in order to fill in those gaps in the  photo now let's take a step away from  stars for a second and talk about  photographing the moon a lot of you have  probably had this frustrating experience  so if you see the moon up in the sky it  looks big and bright and and you see all  the details and then you go to take a  picture of it and it looks like this  it's a tiny bright dot so let's talk  about why this happens and then talk  about a few ways to avoid this to make  the moon look like it looks like to your