  own eyes the reason why this is a  problem is that the moon is relatively  small compared to the things around you  on the ground in the sky and it's also  very bright so your camera especially in  a cell phone is going to have difficulty  exposing for both the dark part of your  images and the bright part of your  images so the moon gets what we call the  blown out it's so bright that we just  can't capture any detail it's also  really small so it's hard to pick out  those details so how do we solve these  problems well the first thing is it's  really small so what I recommend is  taking your photo if you have one  through binoculars or a telescope or if  you have if you have an interchangeable  lens camera use something with a long  length the more that you can zoom into  the moon or into your photo the more  detail you're going to be able to see  this picture in the middle here for  example this was taken through the  telescope with the cellphone and you can  see a lot of detail here in the moon so  this helps us get at the the moon is  very small problem but how do we solve  the brightness problem so even if I can  zoom in here's a picture of the moon and  it's still very bright so how do I get  my image to be darker and you can see in  the image on the right here there's a  lot more detail I can see the kind of  striations near the South Pole of the  moon here and the different types of  rock a lot more is becoming clear so to  do this use a shorter shutter speed so a  shorter shutter speed is going to get  rid of a lot of that excess light and we  can also decrease our ISO and this is  why again it's super important as we  talked about in previous tutorials to  have access to manual settings on  whatever camera you're using by raising  that shutter speed and dropping the ISO  you're going to be able to capture  images of the moon that are much more  detailed than you are able to get before  another tip for photographing the moon  that's actually even easier than  adjusting your settings you can even do  this on auto mode is photograph the moon  during the day during the day you don't  have that huge range of light the  darkness of the sky versus the really  bright moon everything is at a more  level brightness so your cameras going  to be able to expose oh the image a lot  more easily so if you haven't tried it  try taking your image during the day and  you're gonna see a lot more detail than  you do at night if you don't have a  telephoto lens or binoculars around to  wrap up the last thing I would like to  talk about is how to how to add  something a little bit extra to your  photos to make them really interesting  and this is going to be foreground  and it's great to take images of what we  called star fields they're really  interesting you can see all the number  of stars you can look at specific stars  constellations but you can make these  photos a little bit more interesting by  adding some context to them by including  some foreground so let people know where  you are where was this image taken what  do the stars look like relative to where  you are and this can be a little bit  tricky because the sky is really dark  there's not much light on the things  around you but if you expose your camera  and make your settings so that things  close to you are really bright then  you're not going to be able to see the  stars or if you expose for the stars and  the things in the foreground are going  to be way too bright so how do you how  do you control that and then a lot of  times the images that you see that have  foreground in them are actually what are  called composite so what you would do is  take an image of the stars and expose  your set your settings expose for the  sky and then you do a separate photo  that has the correct exposure for what's  in front of you and then you combine  them but there are a few ways to  actually do this without using any  software so what you can see here is I  actually did what is called light  painting and I set my camera up so I'm  exposing for the stars so I use a long  shutter speed in order to capture the  detail in the sky that I want and then  what I do is during that exposure I take  a flashlight that's are in the first  picture I use a flashlight in a second  when I'm using just the light from my  cell phone  and the third one I'm actually using  moonlight the moon was really bright  that night so I couldn't actually see  the Milky Way but it ended up lighting  this castle in a really neat way but  using the ambient light to light the  things around you so if you're using a  flashlight or cell phone what you can do  is just briefly so let's say you're  doing a 30 second exposure turn on that  flashlight shine it  the objects in your foreground that you  want to be able to see in your photo and  then turn it off just like we talked  about in the high speed video your  camera is only going to record light  when light is present so by only turning  your light on and shining it around on  the objects that you want in your  picture and then turning it off you're  going to get the brightness that you  want on the foreground without it being  overexposed you're only going to capture  light when your light sources on and you  can experiment with how long you leave  the light on to get the best results  thank you for joining us for this  tutorial in our imaging science series  with the MIT Museum and Koch Institute  we'd love to see your stunning science  photos if you'd like to share them with  us you can show them on Facebook Twitter  and Instagram and use the hashtag MIT em  imaging science to share those with us  at the end of this program so on June  20th will be holding a webinar to look  at what makes a great science photo and  some of the so submissions have a chance  to be featured during that webinar for  more information on the imaging science  program other tutorials and other  information and resources please visit  the MIT Museum website at the link below  and you will find a list of our other  resources as part of the Imaging Science  program
 

